{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3XKJeG8wKNrc5X4YZV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P8uV41NqkjQKujGG5VuufX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-184630__xa320fb/work/PLAN.md","limit":380},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1965,"cache_read_input_tokens":22572,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1965},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4110026b-2798-4dbf-ae8a-e81d8e816e30","uuid":"1d7e150a-9e49-40a8-8f43-d8e2cf49dd70","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:46.704Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3XK6jcDYmweMbzpLzY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01P8uV41NqkjQKujGG5VuufX":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-184630__xa320fb/work/PLAN.md","limit":380}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P8uV41NqkjQKujGG5VuufX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SNIPEOUT HD\n2\t\n3\t> *Anti-gravity. Anti-everyone.*\n4\t\n5\tAn original anti-gravity combat racer inspired by **Wipeout HD**, built on the my-3D2dge engine (the handoff package\n6\tplus its real-3D add-on). This file is the build plan. **TASK.md** is the brief and **PLAN.md** is how to build it. The\n7\tbuilders may be other AI models working without the planner. Every number here is a starting value, unless this file\n8\tsays a value was checked (the track layouts and jump flights were checked with a script during planning: §4.3, App. C).\n9\t\n10\t**Title.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real random number generator. Fifty riffs\n11\twere brainstormed (none reuses \"Wipeout\" itself). One was drawn with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which reads\n12\tOS entropy, and came out as entry 19 of 50: **Snipeout HD**. The title doubles as the league's slang, kept in the\n13\tfiction: to be *sniped out* is to lose a race to a weapon in its last few hundred metres. The combat mode is called\n14\tSNIPEOUT.\n15\t\n16\t**Names on disk (exact):**\n17\t\n18\t| What | Path (relative to this folder, the one holding TASK.md) |\n19\t| --- | --- |\n20\t| Dev folder (all source, tests, tools) | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728/` |\n21\t| The deliverable, one HTML file | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728.html` |\n22\t| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` (beside the HTML) |\n23\t\n24\tGameTitle = `SnipeoutHD` (the title with spaces removed). SOURCE = `WipeoutHD`.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## 0. How to use this plan\n29\t\n30\t1. Read §1 (the game on one page), then §2 (architecture), then the system you are building in §3. The content you\n31\t   need is in §4.\n32\t2. Build in the order in §5. Each milestone has exit tests. Do not start polish (M12) before every feature in §8 exists.\n33\t3. A system must be complete before it is pretty. The brief forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs in the delivered\n34\t   demo. A small but complete feature beats a large unfinished one. If you must cut, cut **quantity** (props, lines of\n35\t   dialogue, number of songs), never a **feature** listed in §8.\n36\t4. The rules from TASK.md that bind every step:\n37\t   - Keep all files in the dev folder.\n38\t   - Read nothing outside it, except the pinned downloads in §2.1.\n39\t   - Never touch other agents' processes or ports. A server binds `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and is stopped by its own PID.\n40\t   - Testing runs behind one command (§7).\n41\t   - No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n42\t5. **/tmp on this machine is a shared, nearly full tmpfs** (97–99 % used while planning). Point every tool at a temp\n43\t   folder inside the dev folder: `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` for Node, Playwright and Chromium. Put downloads in the dev folder\n44\t   (`vendor/`, `engine/`), never in /tmp.\n45\t\n46\t---\n47\t\n48\t## 1. The game on one page\n49\t\n50\t**Genre:** anti-gravity combat racing (arcade), with a survival score-attack mode (OVERCLOCK) and a combat-points mode\n51\t(SNIPEOUT).\n52\t**Perspective:** third-person chase camera behind the craft. This is the default. △ / C cycles it through a near\n53\tchase, a far chase and a cockpit (nose) view. A look-back button gives a rear view. Trackside \"TV\" cameras are used for\n54\tthe race intros, replays, spectating after elimination, the podium and the attract mode. This is the source's own\n55\tcamera set, and the right one for a racer at 300–800 km/h.\n56\t\n57\t**What makes Wipeout HD distinctive, and where it goes in Snipeout HD:**\n58\t\n59\t| Source trait | Snipeout HD mechanic / UX |\n60\t| --- | --- |\n61\t| Hover craft glued to roller-coaster tracks: banks, crests, drops, jumps, tunnels | Track-space hover physics on a banked 3D ribbon, with gap and drop jumps, tunnels and overpasses (§3.1, §3.2) |\n62\t| Airbrakes as the core skill | Analog left/right airbrakes (L2/R2, Q/E) for tighter turns and slides, and both at once to brake |\n63\t| Pitch control | Nose-down for speed and short jumps, nose-up for crests and long glides |\n64\t| Side-shift and barrel roll | Side-shift on the ground (L1/R1, Z/X). A barrel roll in the air gives a boost but costs shield |\n65\t| Speed pads and weapon pads | Blue chevron pads boost. Amber pads give a random weapon, weighted by position |\n66\t| Absorb | Turn the held weapon into shield energy instead of firing it |\n67\t| Shield energy, pit lane, elimination | Energy is lost to walls, weapons and rolls. A green pit strip recharges it. At 0 the craft is eliminated |\n68\t| Ten weapons | SWARM, SEEKER, MINEFIELD, CANNON, RAILSHOT, TREMOR, BULWARK, AUTOPILOT, NITRO, SIPHON (§3.4) |\n69\t| Speed classes | SPARK → FLARE → STORM → SINGULARITY, each faster (§4.2) |\n70\t| Corporate teams, sleek liveries, graphic-design branding, deadpan announcer | Six original teams (§4.1), fictional sponsors, the Authority's race control, a gibberish-voiced announcer that names every pickup |\n71\t| Zone mode (survive rising speed in an abstract skin) | OVERCLOCK mode with the neon-grid skin (§3.3) |\n72\t| Eliminator | SNIPEOUT mode (points for hits and eliminations, respawns) |\n73\t| Electronic soundtrack | Six original synthesized songs that build in layers (§3.10) |\n74\t| HD presentation: speed, bloom, 60 fps sheen | World3D's bloom, FOV kick, speed lines, exhaust trails, pixel-perfect HUD |\n75\t\n76\t**Modes (all playable in the demo):** QUICK RACE, SEASON (the campaign, with story), TIME TRIAL (ghost), HOT LAP,\n77\tSNIPEOUT, OVERCLOCK, ACADEMY (the tutorial), VERSUS (2-player split screen), REPLAY (from any race's results), ATTRACT\n78\t(the demo), and the AI HUB SESSION (online ghost race, inside the hub only).\n79\t\n80\t**Content:** 4 tracks of 3.8–5.0 km, 6 teams with distinct craft, 12 pilots, 4 speed classes, 10 weapons, 6 songs,\n81\t10 season events plus a 4-race Grand Prix, an 11-drill academy, an ending.\n82\t\n83\t**Controls.** The controller (DualSense, standard mapping) comes first. The keyboard is native. The mouse drives the\n84\tmenus and fires.\n85\t\n86\t| Action | Controller | Keyboard / mouse |\n87\t| --- | --- | --- |\n88\t| Steer | Left stick ←→ (analog), d-pad ←→ | A / D (also ← / →) |\n89\t| Pitch nose down / up | Left stick ↑ / ↓, d-pad ↑ / ↓ | ↑ / ↓ |\n90\t| Thrust (hold) | ✕ | W |\n91\t| Airbrake left / right (analog) | L2 / R2 | Q / E |\n92\t| Both airbrakes (brake) | L2 + R2 | S |\n93\t| Side-shift (ground) / barrel roll (air) | L1 / R1 | Z / X (or double-tap Q / E) |\n94\t| Fire (hold to charge or stream) | □ | Space, left click |\n95\t| Absorb weapon | ○ | Left Shift, right click |\n96\t| Camera | △ | C |\n97\t| Look back (hold) | R3 | B |\n98\t| Pause | Options | Esc / P |\n99\t| Menus | d-pad / stick, ✕ select, ○ back | arrows or WASD, Enter select, Esc / Backspace back, mouse hover + click |\n100\t\n101\t---\n102\t\n103\t## 2. Tech stack and architecture\n104\t\n105\t### 2.1 Stack (pinned)\n106\t\n107\t| Piece | Version | Where it comes from (download into the dev folder) |\n108\t| --- | --- | --- |\n109\t| my-3D2dge engine, full edition (runs the game: scenes, input, menus, settings, audio, fonts, rigs, HUD) | v0.85.2 | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`, pinned at commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`. Copy `dist/handoff/` into `engine/handoff/` (the docs `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the kits and the tools). The game loads `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, a copy of `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` with the edits in §2.7 |\n110\t| my-3D2dge 3D add-on (World3D: the three.js renderer under the game's canvas, terrain, lights, fog, bloom/pixel filters, 3D particles, cameras, split-screen cameras) | v0.85.2 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` → `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`. Its header is its manual: read lines 1–260 |\n111\t| three.js (WebGPURenderer, falls back to WebGL 2 by itself) | r182 (`three@0.182.0`) | CDN: `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `.../three.tsl.min.js` (verified 200, CORS `*`) |\n112\t| Rapier (World3D needs it to start; ships do not use it) | 0.19.3 | CDN: `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (verified: one self-contained ESM with the WASM inlined, default export) |\n113\t| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/lib/hub/session.js` → `vendor/aihub-session.js`, **pasted unmodified** |\n114\t| AI Hub harness, for tests only, never bundled | same commit | `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` → `tests/hub/` |\n115\t| Playwright (browser tests only) | 1.63.0 (the handoff's pin) | `npm install` in the dev folder. Chromium 1243 is already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` on this machine |\n116\t\n117\t**Fetch (verified during planning).** Run from the dev folder. Everything lands inside it:\n118\t\n119\t```sh\n120\tmkdir -p .tmp engine vendor tests/hub && export TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp\n121\tgit init -q .tmp/my3d && git -C .tmp/my3d remote add origin https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge\n122\tgit -C .tmp/my3d sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n123\tgit -C .tmp/my3d fetch -q --depth 1 --filter=blob:none origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72\n124\tgit -C .tmp/my3d checkout -q FETCH_HEAD\n125\tcp -r .tmp/my3d/dist/handoff engine/handoff && cp engine/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\n126\tR=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72\n127\tcurl -fsSL $R/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js -o engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js\n128\tH=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/lib/hub\n129\tcurl -fsSL $H/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\n130\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSL $H/$f -o tests/hub/$f; done\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\tWhy the 3D add-on: Wipeout's identity is three-dimensional (banks, crests, drops, overpasses, a chase camera that rolls\n134\twith the track). The handoff's 2D views cannot show that: Mode 7 is flat, and the road module has no true layout. The\n135\thandoff README itself points 3D games at this add-on. It keeps the engine's native look: pixel-resolution rendering\n136\t(`res: 'pixels'`), its filters, its toon/lambert lighting, its 3D particles, rigs drawn as cards, and the 2D engine's\n137\tpixel HUD, fonts and menus on top. Everything else in the game is the handoff engine.\n138\t\n139\tRead in the handoff package: `API.md` (whole), then `AI_GUIDE.md` §Standard features, §Frame order, §Players and\n140\t§Testing. Read the World3D header whole. Search `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole. Useful kits to skim:\n141\t`kits/kart.js` (E.Race usage, split screen) and the repo's `src/starter3d/50-racing.js` (World3D racing:\n142\t`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/src/starter3d/50-racing.js`).\n143\t\n144\t### 2.2 Dev folder layout\n145\t\n146\t```\n147\tSnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728/\n148\t  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" } }\n149\t  index.html              dev page (separate files; serve it: the 3D add-on is an ES module, so file:// can't load it as src)\n150\t  engine/\n151\t    my-3d2dge.js          the handoff engine + our edits (§2.7), each edit marked  // SNIPEOUT EDIT: <why>\n152\t    my-3d2dge-3d.js       the 3D add-on + our edits, marked the same way\n153\t    handoff/              pristine copy of dist/handoff (docs, kits, tools) for reference and the tools\n154\t  vendor/aihub-session.js the hub client, byte-identical to the pinned file\n155\t  src/                    the game: plain scripts (no import/export), loaded in this order (src/manifest.json lists it)\n156\t    00-core.js            SN namespace, constants, units, math (vec3, frames), RNG streams, hash, Tune registry, event bus\n157\t    01-input.js           SN.INPUT preset, control mapping (input + virtual pad -> ShipControls), double-tap, flick detect\n158\t    05-data-teams.js      teams, craft specs, pilots, principals, speed classes\n159\t    06-data-weapons.js    weapon definitions and pickup tables\n160\t    07-data-tracks.js     the four TrackDefs (§4.3), themes\n161\t    08-data-season.js     season tiers, events, Grand Prix, unlock rules\n162\t    09-data-story.js      every line of dialogue and announcer callout (§4.5)\n163\t    10-data-music.js      the songs and stings (engine tracker format + our extensions)\n164\t    11-data-about.js      SN.ABOUT: source info, feedback (feeds the About screen AND exit-interview.json)\n165\t    20-track.js           TrackSpline, Track (slices, frames, features, queries, racing line, speed profile)\n166\t    25-ship.js            Ship state and the physics step (§3.2)\n167\t    27-collide.js         ship-ship collisions\n168\t    30-weapons.js         pickups, projectiles, mines, beams, waves, effects on ships, absorb\n169\t    35-ai.js              AI pilots (line following, speed control, overtaking, pads, weapons, rubber band)\n170\t    40-race.js            Race: lifecycle, laps, sectors, positions, finish, modes' rules, rescue, results\n171\t    45-replay.js          input recording, deterministic replay, ghost recording/playback, records\n172\t    50-audio.js           synth extensions use, engine hum, SFX catalogue, music director, gibberish voice\n173\t    55-glyphs.js          button/key/mouse prompt icons, PlayStation and Xbox sets\n174\t    60-render.js          World3D world per track, materials, interpolation, cameras, TV director, split screen\n175\t    62-shipkit.js         procedural craft meshes, liveries, animation (flaps, glow, roll), ghosts, explosions\n176\t    64-trackmesh.js       track ribbon meshes: surface, walls, neon, pads, tunnels, supports, gantries, overclock skin\n177\t    66-scenery.js         theme generators: terrain height/color, buildings, rocks, water, aurora, crowds of props\n178\t    68-fx.js              weapon FX, trails, sparks, speed lines, shield flashes, rescue drone, weather\n179\t    70-hud.js             race HUD (all modes), split-screen HUD, callouts, name tags, reticles, minimap\n180\t    75-ui.js              10-foot widget kit (focus, nav, hover, click, prompts) and every front-end screen\n181\t    78-season-ui.js       season hub, briefings (E.Dialog + portraits + voice), GP standings, ending\n182\t    80-dev.js             dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, reset, debug overlays\n183\t    85-attract.js         idle detection, attract playlist, demo cheats\n184\t    90-hub.js             AI Hub session: connect, own card, ghost send/receive, progress/result/time-up/end\n185\t    95-main.js            new E.Game, scenes, boot steps, wiring, game.debug\n186\t  tests/\n187\t    run.mjs               THE one command (§7)\n188\t    lib/vm.mjs            loads engine + src/ into a Node vm context (headless game)\n189\t    lib/bot.mjs           player bot (drives a human ship through the virtual pad)\n190\t    unit/ physics/ race/ weapons/ modes/ flow/ season/ hub/  (Node suites, *.test.mjs)\n191\t    browser/*.test.mjs    Playwright suites (bundle loads, renders, screenshots, gamepad stub)\n192\t    hub/                  harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (pinned copies) + hub.test.mjs\n193\t    out/                  screenshots and reports (git-ignored)\n194\t  tools/\n195\t    bundle.mjs            builds ../SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728.html\n196\t    trackcheck.mjs        track validator (App. C algorithm), prints the §4.3 numbers\n197\t    medals.mjs            measures the bot's best times per track/class and writes the medal targets (§4.2)\n198\t    exit-interview.mjs    writes ../exit-interview.json from src/11-data-about.js and checks it parses\n199\t```\n200\t\n201\t### 2.3 The page and the bundle\n202\t\n203\tScript order inside the single HTML file (`tools/bundle.mjs` writes it; everything is inline):\n204\t\n205\t```html\n206\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n207\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n208\t<script>/* vendor/aihub-session.js, byte-identical (the hub's rule: first script, right after meta charset) */</script>\n209\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n210\t<title>Snipeout HD</title>\n211\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n212\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\"> <meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n213\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Finish 3 laps first: fastest time wins\">\n214\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Steer A/D or stick · Thrust W or ✕ · Airbrakes Q/E or L2/R2 · Fire Space or □\">\n215\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"> <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n216\t<style>/* as the handoff template: html/body 100%, #stage, #view under #screen, image-rendering: pixelated */</style>\n217\t<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n218\t  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n219\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n220\t  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n221\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n222\t</head><body>\n223\t<div id=\"e3-boot\">…the handoff template's static boot splash, title SNIPEOUT HD…</div>\n224\t<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n225\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n226\t<script>/* src/*.js in manifest order, concatenated, each prefixed with a  // ==== src/<file> ====  marker (classic) */</script>\n227\t<script type=\"module\" onerror=\"window.__sn3dFail='load'\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (ES module, inline) */</script>\n228\t</body></html>\n229\t```\n230\t\n231\t- The game is **classic scripts**. They run first, build the game and call `game.start({ boot })`. The 3D add-on is a\n232\t  deferred module, so it finishes later.\n233\t- Boot step 1 (`'Waking the 3D engine'`) returns a promise. It polls for `My3D2dge.World3D` every 50 ms, then\n234\t  `await E.World3D.init(game)`. If the add-on has not appeared after 30 s, or `__sn3dFail` is set, the step throws\n235\t  `Error('The 3D engine could not load (three.js from cdn.jsdelivr.net). Check the connection and reload.')`. The\n236\t  engine's boot screen shows that error. Headless (Node), the step returns at once and no renderer is made.\n237\t- **Every `src/` file is one IIFE**, `(() => { const E = My3D2dge, SN = globalThis.SN; … })();`. It registers what\n238\t  it exports on `globalThis.SN`. Top-level `const`/`let` would collide once the files are concatenated into one\n239\t  script, and in the vm context.\n240\t- **No file may touch `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` or `E.World3D` at top level.** Only call them inside functions that run after\n241\t  the boot step. This keeps every file loadable in Node for the tests.\n242\t- The dev page (`index.html`) loads the same files separately. Serve it with\n243\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the dev folder (note the PID, stop it yourself). Day-to-day checks\n244\t  use the bundle, which works from `file://`. The CDN imports work cross-origin: jsDelivr sends\n245\t  `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, verified. Chromium treats file:// as a secure context. Wherever WebGPU is missing,\n246\t  three.js falls back to WebGL 2 by itself.\n247\t- `tools/bundle.mjs` also checks that the inlined hub client equals `vendor/aihub-session.js` byte for byte, that no\n248\t  source contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX` or `placeholder`, and that no `<script src=` remains. It prints the file size.\n249\t\n250\t### 2.4 Units, axes, conventions (bugs hide here: unit-test them first)\n251\t\n252\t- **Engine units everywhere in the sim:** 16 u = 1 m (World3D's `U`). Speeds are u/s, accelerations u/s². Track data\n253\t  is written in **metres** and converted once, at load (`×16`). Displayed speed: `km/h = v[u/s] × 0.225`\n254\t  (= 3.6 / 16).\n255\t- **Axes (the engine's):** x east, y south, z up. This frame is *left-handed*. World3D converts to three.js with\n256\t  `toThree(x, y, z) = (x/16, z/16, y/16)`.\n257\t- **Angles:** radians from +x toward +y (0 = east, π/2 = south). World3D's camera yaw uses the same.\n258\t- **Frames:** for a forward vector `F` and an up vector `U`, **right `R = cross(U, F)`** with the standard cross-product\n259\t  formula. Check: F = (1,0,0) east, U = (0,0,1) gives R = (0,1,0) south, which is the right-hand side when heading\n260\t  east. Then `U = cross(F, R)`. Write `SN.frame(F, Uhint)` once and unit-test it on 8 headings.\n261\t- **Bank:** a positive bank rolls the right edge down. A right-hand turn (heading angle increasing) gets a positive\n262\t  bank. Banked `R' = R·cos b − U·sin b` and `U' = U·cos b + R·sin b`. Unit test: in a right turn the right edge is lower\n263\t  (`z` of `C + R'·w` < `z` of `C − R'·w`).\n264\t- **Time:** the engine's fixed step, 1/120 s (`update(dt)`). The sim never reads wall-clock time. `game.alpha` is only\n265\t  for drawing.\n266\t- **Randomness:** the sim uses `SN.rng(stream)` (wrapping `E.rng(seed)`), one stream per purpose:\n267\t  `race.rng('pickup')` and `race.rng('ai:' + i)`. **Never `Math.random` in the sim.** Cosmetic code (particles,\n268\t  confetti) may use it.\n269\t- **Track coordinates:** `s` is arc length along the centre line in u, 0 at the start line, wrapping at `L`. `u` is\n270\t  lateral, positive to the right (along `R'`). `h` is height above the surface, along `U'`. A ship's race progress is\n271\t  `p = lap·L + s`.\n272\t\n273\t### 2.5 Modules and their public surfaces\n274\t\n275\tThe sim layer (Node-safe, deterministic): `00-core, 01-input (mapping only), 05–11 data, 20-track, 25-ship,\n276\t27-collide, 30-weapons, 35-ai, 40-race, 45-replay`. The presentation layer (browser only, reads the sim, never writes\n277\tto it): `50-audio, 55-glyphs, 60–68 render/fx, 70-hud, 75/78-ui, 80-dev (UI side), 85-attract (director side),\n278\t90-hub (network side)`.\n279\t\n280\tThe sim talks to presentation through **`race.events`**: an array the race clears at the start of each step and fills\n281\tduring it. Examples: `{ t: 'pickup', ship, weapon }`, `{ t: 'fire', ship, weapon }`, `{ t: 'hit', ship, by, weapon,\n282\tdmg }`, `{ t: 'wall', ship, impact }`, `{ t: 'scrape', ship, k }`, `{ t: 'pad', ship, kind }`, `{ t: 'land', ship,\n283\tangle }`, `{ t: 'roll', ship, ok }`, `{ t: 'lap', ship, lap, time }`, `{ t: 'finish', ship, place, time }`,\n284\t`{ t: 'elim', ship, by }`, `{ t: 'respawn', ship }`, `{ t: 'rescue', ship }`, `{ t: 'wrongway', ship }`,\n285\t`{ t: 'lock', ship, target }`, `{ t: 'zone', n }`, `{ t: 'drill', id, ok }`. Presentation reads the events every\n286\tstep for sounds, particles, HUD callouts and rumble. Tests assert on them.\n287\t\n288\tKey APIs, as a contract the builders keep:\n289\t\n290\t```js\n291\tconst T = SN.Track.build(SN.TRACKS.solenne)     // throws with a clear message if the data is invalid\n292\tT.L; T.slices                                    // slice i at s = i*T.ds (ds ≈ 32 u)\n293\tT.frameAt(s, out)                                // { C, F, R, U, w (half width), bank, flags } interpolated\n294\tT.surface(s, u, out)                             // { P (surface point), N (normal), wallL, wallR, kind: 'deck'|'gap'|'drop', ceil }\n295\tT.locate(P, sHint)                               // local Newton search from sHint -> { s, u, h } (never global)\n296\tT.query(x, y, zHint)                             // 2D bucket lookup -> nearest slice with surface below zHint (particles, terrain, cameras)\n297\tT.groundBelow(x, y, z)                           // the track surface z under (x, y) below z (via query), else -Infinity: World3D's particleGround\n298\tT.line(s) / T.vline(s)                           // AI racing line lateral offset (u) and target speed (u/s) per class\n299\tT.features                                       // pads, pit, tunnels, jumps, openEdges, sectors, grid slots, start\n300\tconst R = new SN.Race({ track: T, mode, cls, laps, seed, entrants, rules })   // entrants: [{ pilot, team, control: 'human'|'ai'|'ghost', input }]\n301\tR.step(dt); R.ships; R.events; R.state; R.results(); R.hash()\n302\tSN.Ship.step(ship, controls, dt, race)           // the physics step (§3.2)\n303\tSN.AI.controls(ship, race) -> ShipControls       // also used by the player bot, autopilot and attract\n304\tSN.Replay.record(race) / SN.Replay.play(data)    // deterministic re-simulation\n305\tSN.Tune.T.<key>                                  // live gameplay variables (§3.15), SN.Tune.reset()\n306\t```\n307\t\n308\t`ShipControls = { steer: -1..1, pitch: -1..1 (+ = nose down), thrust: 0|1, brakeL: 0..1, brakeR: 0..1, fire: bool\n309\t(held), firePressed, fireReleased, absorb: bool (pressed), shiftL, shiftR: bool (pressed) }`.\n310\t\n311\t### 2.6 Scenes (engine scenes) and the flow\n312\t\n313\t```\n314\tboot steps ──► title ──(10 s idle)──► attract ──(any input)──► title\n315\t                 │ (touch-only: notice + SETTINGS button; attract still runs)\n316\t                 ▼ START\n317\t               menu ──► setup (class → track → team/pilot → laps) ──► loading ──► race ──► results ──► replay\n318\t                 │                                                                  ▲        │\n319\t                 ├──► season (tiers, events, briefings, GP standings, ending) ──────┘        └──► menu / retry / next\n320\t                 ├──► academy (a race mode) · time trial · hot lap · snipeout · overclock · versus (join P2)\n321\t                 └──► how to play · settings (engine) · about/credits\n322\tAIHub.active ──► session (own card ─► race ─► round over)   (no title, menus or attract)\n323\t```\n324\t\n325\t- `race` is the only `pausable: true` scene. It is not pausable in a hub session. Its pause menu is the engine's.\n326\t  **`menu.items` may be a function**: the engine calls it each time the pause screen opens and drops falsy entries\n327\t  (verified in the engine: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`), so the items can depend on the\n328\t  mode. It has: Resume (engine), **Restart**, **Camera** (cycles), **How to play**, **Dev mode**, Settings,\n329\t  Controls, Full screen (engine), and Quit (engine, `quit: 'menu'`; `false` in a session). In Overclock and Hot Lap it\n330\t  also has **End run**, which counts as finishing.\n331\t- Every scene's `draw(r)` calls `world.draw(r)` for the 3D picture first (the active world: the hangar for menus, the\n332\t  track for races and the title), then its 2D UI in `r.overlay`.\n333\t- `game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(s, u), … }` (§7) exposes the state for tests and `tools/check.mjs`.\n334\t\n335\t### 2.7 Engine changes (make them in M0–M2; mark each `// SNIPEOUT EDIT:`)\n336\t\n337\t1. **Button symbols default to PlayStation, and the option lives in Settings.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs`\n338\t   (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`): `group: 'Display'`, `value: 'playstation'`,\n339\t   `options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox', 'Xbox A B X Y']]`.\n340\t2. **Font glyphs ✕ ○ □ △** in both pixel fonts (the `G57` 5×7 table and the `GLYPHS` 3×5 table), so `E.font.text`\n341\t   can print `input.label()` results. The pretty colored prompt icons are our own (`55-glyphs.js`). These are the\n342\t   fallback inside running text.\n343\t3. **Analog buttons.** In `Input.tick`, store `this.padValues[i] = b.value` and add\n344\t   `input.value(action) → 0..1`: the largest over its bound codes. Keys and mouse give 1 while held. Pad buttons give\n345\t   their `value`. Axis codes give the stick magnitude. Tests can set `input._valueOverride[action]`. Airbrakes use it\n346\t   (L2/R2 analog).\n347\t4. **Synth extensions in `Audio._voice` / `_note`** (search `_voice(p, bus, t0, mul = 1)`). Oscillator voices accept:\n348\t   - `lp: { f, q, env, dec }`: a lowpass BiquadFilter at `f` Hz with resonance `q`, opened by `env` Hz that decays over\n349\t     `dec` s (acid bass, plucks);\n350\t   - `uni: n, det: cents`: n detuned copies (supersaws);\n351\t   - `pan: −1..1`.\n352\t\n353\t   Music tracks pass them through: `{ wave: 'saw', lp: {…}, uni: 3, det: 12, pan }`. A note token ending in `!`\n354\t   (`'A2!'`) is an accent: louder, and the filter env doubled. Add drum tokens `p` (clap: three 10 ms noise bursts and\n355\t   a tail) and `b` (sub boom: sine 55→35 Hz, 0.4 s). Sidechain: `duck: .35` on a drums track dips the music bus by\n356\t   that much on every `k` (a 120 ms release). The house and trance songs use it.\n357\t5. **World3D particle ground.** In `World3D.draw` (search `g.particles.ground = (x, y, z) => this.level.floorAt`),\n358\t   use `this.o.particleGround || ((x, y, z) => this.level.floorAt(x, y, z))`. We pass `(x, y, z) => track surface\n359\t   below`, so sparks land on the track, not on the terrain 30 m below.\n360\t6. **(If needed) logarithmic depth.** If distant coplanar geometry z-fights, pass `logarithmicDepthBuffer: true` to\n361\t   `new THREE.WebGPURenderer` in `init`. Try polygon offset on the decal materials first (§3.7).\n362\t7. **Hub rule: Create + Options.** In a session the race is not pausable at all (`pausable: false`), so Options never\n363\t   opens our pause. No engine change is needed. Note it in the About screen.\n364\t\n365\tRecord every change and its reason in `SN.ABOUT.engineChanges` (shown in About and the exit interview).\n366\t\n367\t### 2.8 Determinism rules\n368\t\n369\t- The sim depends only on the seed, the track and class, the Tune values (snapshotted into each replay), and per-step\n370\t  controls. Ships update in grid order and pairs collide in index order.\n371\t- `R.hash()` is FNV-1a over the Float32 bits of every ship's `(p, u, h, |v|, energy, lap)` and every projectile's\n372\t  `(s, u)`, the same algorithm as World3D's `hashNumbers`.\n373\t- Each race records `{ seed, track, cls, mode, laps, entrants, tune, frames[] }`, with `frames` holding the human\n374\t  controls per step. Pack them as Int8 steer/pitch, Uint8 brakes, and a bit field. Run-length encode repeated frames.\n375\t  Replaying must reproduce `R.hash()` at the end (a test).\n376\t- AI uses its own RNG stream. Rigs and particles may use `Math.random` because they never feed back into the sim.\n377\t\n378\t---\n379\t## 3. Systems\n380\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4110026b-2798-4dbf-ae8a-e81d8e816e30","uuid":"05231a34-9b3b-440f-90eb-a4068008031f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:46:46.880Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261103-184630__xa320fb/work/PLAN.md","content":"# SNIPEOUT HD\n\n> *Anti-gravity. Anti-everyone.*\n\nAn original anti-gravity combat racer inspired by **Wipeout HD**, built on the my-3D2dge engine (the handoff package\nplus its real-3D add-on). This file is the build plan. **TASK.md** is the brief and **PLAN.md** is how to build it. The\nbuilders may be other AI models working without the planner. Every number here is a starting value, unless this file\nsays a value was checked (the track layouts and jump flights were checked with a script during planning: §4.3, App. C).\n\n**Title.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, picked by a real random number generator. Fifty riffs\nwere brainstormed (none reuses \"Wipeout\" itself). One was drawn with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)`, which reads\nOS entropy, and came out as entry 19 of 50: **Snipeout HD**. The title doubles as the league's slang, kept in the\nfiction: to be *sniped out* is to lose a race to a weapon in its last few hundred metres. The combat mode is called\nSNIPEOUT.\n\n**Names on disk (exact):**\n\n| What | Path (relative to this folder, the one holding TASK.md) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dev folder (all source, tests, tools) | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728/` |\n| The deliverable, one HTML file | `SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728.html` |\n| Exit interview | `exit-interview.json` (beside the HTML) |\n\nGameTitle = `SnipeoutHD` (the title with spaces removed). SOURCE = `WipeoutHD`.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n1. Read §1 (the game on one page), then §2 (architecture), then the system you are building in §3. The content you\n   need is in §4.\n2. Build in the order in §5. Each milestone has exit tests. Do not start polish (M12) before every feature in §8 exists.\n3. A system must be complete before it is pretty. The brief forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs in the delivered\n   demo. A small but complete feature beats a large unfinished one. If you must cut, cut **quantity** (props, lines of\n   dialogue, number of songs), never a **feature** listed in §8.\n4. The rules from TASK.md that bind every step:\n   - Keep all files in the dev folder.\n   - Read nothing outside it, except the pinned downloads in §2.1.\n   - Never touch other agents' processes or ports. A server binds `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and is stopped by its own PID.\n   - Testing runs behind one command (§7).\n   - No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n5. **/tmp on this machine is a shared, nearly full tmpfs** (97–99 % used while planning). Point every tool at a temp\n   folder inside the dev folder: `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` for Node, Playwright and Chromium. Put downloads in the dev folder\n   (`vendor/`, `engine/`), never in /tmp.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Genre:** anti-gravity combat racing (arcade), with a survival score-attack mode (OVERCLOCK) and a combat-points mode\n(SNIPEOUT).\n**Perspective:** third-person chase camera behind the craft. This is the default. △ / C cycles it through a near\nchase, a far chase and a cockpit (nose) view. A look-back button gives a rear view. Trackside \"TV\" cameras are used for\nthe race intros, replays, spectating after elimination, the podium and the attract mode. This is the source's own\ncamera set, and the right one for a racer at 300–800 km/h.\n\n**What makes Wipeout HD distinctive, and where it goes in Snipeout HD:**\n\n| Source trait | Snipeout HD mechanic / UX |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hover craft glued to roller-coaster tracks: banks, crests, drops, jumps, tunnels | Track-space hover physics on a banked 3D ribbon, with gap and drop jumps, tunnels and overpasses (§3.1, §3.2) |\n| Airbrakes as the core skill | Analog left/right airbrakes (L2/R2, Q/E) for tighter turns and slides, and both at once to brake |\n| Pitch control | Nose-down for speed and short jumps, nose-up for crests and long glides |\n| Side-shift and barrel roll | Side-shift on the ground (L1/R1, Z/X). A barrel roll in the air gives a boost but costs shield |\n| Speed pads and weapon pads | Blue chevron pads boost. Amber pads give a random weapon, weighted by position |\n| Absorb | Turn the held weapon into shield energy instead of firing it |\n| Shield energy, pit lane, elimination | Energy is lost to walls, weapons and rolls. A green pit strip recharges it. At 0 the craft is eliminated |\n| Ten weapons | SWARM, SEEKER, MINEFIELD, CANNON, RAILSHOT, TREMOR, BULWARK, AUTOPILOT, NITRO, SIPHON (§3.4) |\n| Speed classes | SPARK → FLARE → STORM → SINGULARITY, each faster (§4.2) |\n| Corporate teams, sleek liveries, graphic-design branding, deadpan announcer | Six original teams (§4.1), fictional sponsors, the Authority's race control, a gibberish-voiced announcer that names every pickup |\n| Zone mode (survive rising speed in an abstract skin) | OVERCLOCK mode with the neon-grid skin (§3.3) |\n| Eliminator | SNIPEOUT mode (points for hits and eliminations, respawns) |\n| Electronic soundtrack | Six original synthesized songs that build in layers (§3.10) |\n| HD presentation: speed, bloom, 60 fps sheen | World3D's bloom, FOV kick, speed lines, exhaust trails, pixel-perfect HUD |\n\n**Modes (all playable in the demo):** QUICK RACE, SEASON (the campaign, with story), TIME TRIAL (ghost), HOT LAP,\nSNIPEOUT, OVERCLOCK, ACADEMY (the tutorial), VERSUS (2-player split screen), REPLAY (from any race's results), ATTRACT\n(the demo), and the AI HUB SESSION (online ghost race, inside the hub only).\n\n**Content:** 4 tracks of 3.8–5.0 km, 6 teams with distinct craft, 12 pilots, 4 speed classes, 10 weapons, 6 songs,\n10 season events plus a 4-race Grand Prix, an 11-drill academy, an ending.\n\n**Controls.** The controller (DualSense, standard mapping) comes first. The keyboard is native. The mouse drives the\nmenus and fires.\n\n| Action | Controller | Keyboard / mouse |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Steer | Left stick ←→ (analog), d-pad ←→ | A / D (also ← / →) |\n| Pitch nose down / up | Left stick ↑ / ↓, d-pad ↑ / ↓ | ↑ / ↓ |\n| Thrust (hold) | ✕ | W |\n| Airbrake left / right (analog) | L2 / R2 | Q / E |\n| Both airbrakes (brake) | L2 + R2 | S |\n| Side-shift (ground) / barrel roll (air) | L1 / R1 | Z / X (or double-tap Q / E) |\n| Fire (hold to charge or stream) | □ | Space, left click |\n| Absorb weapon | ○ | Left Shift, right click |\n| Camera | △ | C |\n| Look back (hold) | R3 | B |\n| Pause | Options | Esc / P |\n| Menus | d-pad / stick, ✕ select, ○ back | arrows or WASD, Enter select, Esc / Backspace back, mouse hover + click |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tech stack and architecture\n\n### 2.1 Stack (pinned)\n\n| Piece | Version | Where it comes from (download into the dev folder) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| my-3D2dge engine, full edition (runs the game: scenes, input, menus, settings, audio, fonts, rigs, HUD) | v0.85.2 | `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`, pinned at commit `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`. Copy `dist/handoff/` into `engine/handoff/` (the docs `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the kits and the tools). The game loads `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, a copy of `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` with the edits in §2.7 |\n| my-3D2dge 3D add-on (World3D: the three.js renderer under the game's canvas, terrain, lights, fog, bloom/pixel filters, 3D particles, cameras, split-screen cameras) | v0.85.2 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` → `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`. Its header is its manual: read lines 1–260 |\n| three.js (WebGPURenderer, falls back to WebGL 2 by itself) | r182 (`three@0.182.0`) | CDN: `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `.../three.tsl.min.js` (verified 200, CORS `*`) |\n| Rapier (World3D needs it to start; ships do not use it) | 0.19.3 | CDN: `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (verified: one self-contained ESM with the WASM inlined, default export) |\n| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/lib/hub/session.js` → `vendor/aihub-session.js`, **pasted unmodified** |\n| AI Hub harness, for tests only, never bundled | same commit | `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` → `tests/hub/` |\n| Playwright (browser tests only) | 1.63.0 (the handoff's pin) | `npm install` in the dev folder. Chromium 1243 is already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` on this machine |\n\n**Fetch (verified during planning).** Run from the dev folder. Everything lands inside it:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p .tmp engine vendor tests/hub && export TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp\ngit init -q .tmp/my3d && git -C .tmp/my3d remote add origin https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge\ngit -C .tmp/my3d sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ngit -C .tmp/my3d fetch -q --depth 1 --filter=blob:none origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72\ngit -C .tmp/my3d checkout -q FETCH_HEAD\ncp -r .tmp/my3d/dist/handoff engine/handoff && cp engine/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\nR=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72\ncurl -fsSL $R/engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js -o engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js\nH=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/lib/hub\ncurl -fsSL $H/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\nfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSL $H/$f -o tests/hub/$f; done\n```\n\nWhy the 3D add-on: Wipeout's identity is three-dimensional (banks, crests, drops, overpasses, a chase camera that rolls\nwith the track). The handoff's 2D views cannot show that: Mode 7 is flat, and the road module has no true layout. The\nhandoff README itself points 3D games at this add-on. It keeps the engine's native look: pixel-resolution rendering\n(`res: 'pixels'`), its filters, its toon/lambert lighting, its 3D particles, rigs drawn as cards, and the 2D engine's\npixel HUD, fonts and menus on top. Everything else in the game is the handoff engine.\n\nRead in the handoff package: `API.md` (whole), then `AI_GUIDE.md` §Standard features, §Frame order, §Players and\n§Testing. Read the World3D header whole. Search `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole. Useful kits to skim:\n`kits/kart.js` (E.Race usage, split screen) and the repo's `src/starter3d/50-racing.js` (World3D racing:\n`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/src/starter3d/50-racing.js`).\n\n### 2.2 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nSnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728/\n  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" } }\n  index.html              dev page (separate files; serve it: the 3D add-on is an ES module, so file:// can't load it as src)\n  engine/\n    my-3d2dge.js          the handoff engine + our edits (§2.7), each edit marked  // SNIPEOUT EDIT: <why>\n    my-3d2dge-3d.js       the 3D add-on + our edits, marked the same way\n    handoff/              pristine copy of dist/handoff (docs, kits, tools) for reference and the tools\n  vendor/aihub-session.js the hub client, byte-identical to the pinned file\n  src/                    the game: plain scripts (no import/export), loaded in this order (src/manifest.json lists it)\n    00-core.js            SN namespace, constants, units, math (vec3, frames), RNG streams, hash, Tune registry, event bus\n    01-input.js           SN.INPUT preset, control mapping (input + virtual pad -> ShipControls), double-tap, flick detect\n    05-data-teams.js      teams, craft specs, pilots, principals, speed classes\n    06-data-weapons.js    weapon definitions and pickup tables\n    07-data-tracks.js     the four TrackDefs (§4.3), themes\n    08-data-season.js     season tiers, events, Grand Prix, unlock rules\n    09-data-story.js      every line of dialogue and announcer callout (§4.5)\n    10-data-music.js      the songs and stings (engine tracker format + our extensions)\n    11-data-about.js      SN.ABOUT: source info, feedback (feeds the About screen AND exit-interview.json)\n    20-track.js           TrackSpline, Track (slices, frames, features, queries, racing line, speed profile)\n    25-ship.js            Ship state and the physics step (§3.2)\n    27-collide.js         ship-ship collisions\n    30-weapons.js         pickups, projectiles, mines, beams, waves, effects on ships, absorb\n    35-ai.js              AI pilots (line following, speed control, overtaking, pads, weapons, rubber band)\n    40-race.js            Race: lifecycle, laps, sectors, positions, finish, modes' rules, rescue, results\n    45-replay.js          input recording, deterministic replay, ghost recording/playback, records\n    50-audio.js           synth extensions use, engine hum, SFX catalogue, music director, gibberish voice\n    55-glyphs.js          button/key/mouse prompt icons, PlayStation and Xbox sets\n    60-render.js          World3D world per track, materials, interpolation, cameras, TV director, split screen\n    62-shipkit.js         procedural craft meshes, liveries, animation (flaps, glow, roll), ghosts, explosions\n    64-trackmesh.js       track ribbon meshes: surface, walls, neon, pads, tunnels, supports, gantries, overclock skin\n    66-scenery.js         theme generators: terrain height/color, buildings, rocks, water, aurora, crowds of props\n    68-fx.js              weapon FX, trails, sparks, speed lines, shield flashes, rescue drone, weather\n    70-hud.js             race HUD (all modes), split-screen HUD, callouts, name tags, reticles, minimap\n    75-ui.js              10-foot widget kit (focus, nav, hover, click, prompts) and every front-end screen\n    78-season-ui.js       season hub, briefings (E.Dialog + portraits + voice), GP standings, ending\n    80-dev.js             dev mode panel, cheats, sliders, reset, debug overlays\n    85-attract.js         idle detection, attract playlist, demo cheats\n    90-hub.js             AI Hub session: connect, own card, ghost send/receive, progress/result/time-up/end\n    95-main.js            new E.Game, scenes, boot steps, wiring, game.debug\n  tests/\n    run.mjs               THE one command (§7)\n    lib/vm.mjs            loads engine + src/ into a Node vm context (headless game)\n    lib/bot.mjs           player bot (drives a human ship through the virtual pad)\n    unit/ physics/ race/ weapons/ modes/ flow/ season/ hub/  (Node suites, *.test.mjs)\n    browser/*.test.mjs    Playwright suites (bundle loads, renders, screenshots, gamepad stub)\n    hub/                  harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (pinned copies) + hub.test.mjs\n    out/                  screenshots and reports (git-ignored)\n  tools/\n    bundle.mjs            builds ../SnipeoutHD-WipeoutHD-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174728.html\n    trackcheck.mjs        track validator (App. C algorithm), prints the §4.3 numbers\n    medals.mjs            measures the bot's best times per track/class and writes the medal targets (§4.2)\n    exit-interview.mjs    writes ../exit-interview.json from src/11-data-about.js and checks it parses\n```\n\n### 2.3 The page and the bundle\n\nScript order inside the single HTML file (`tools/bundle.mjs` writes it; everything is inline):\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* vendor/aihub-session.js, byte-identical (the hub's rule: first script, right after meta charset) */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Snipeout HD</title>\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\"> <meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Finish 3 laps first: fastest time wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Steer A/D or stick · Thrust W or ✕ · Airbrakes Q/E or L2/R2 · Fire Space or □\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"> <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<style>/* as the handoff template: html/body 100%, #stage, #view under #screen, image-rendering: pixelated */</style>\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n</head><body>\n<div id=\"e3-boot\">…the handoff template's static boot splash, title SNIPEOUT HD…</div>\n<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n<script>/* src/*.js in manifest order, concatenated, each prefixed with a  // ==== src/<file> ====  marker (classic) */</script>\n<script type=\"module\" onerror=\"window.__sn3dFail='load'\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (ES module, inline) */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\n- The game is **classic scripts**. They run first, build the game and call `game.start({ boot })`. The 3D add-on is a\n  deferred module, so it finishes later.\n- Boot step 1 (`'Waking the 3D engine'`) returns a promise. It polls for `My3D2dge.World3D` every 50 ms, then\n  `await E.World3D.init(game)`. If the add-on has not appeared after 30 s, or `__sn3dFail` is set, the step throws\n  `Error('The 3D engine could not load (three.js from cdn.jsdelivr.net). Check the connection and reload.')`. The\n  engine's boot screen shows that error. Headless (Node), the step returns at once and no renderer is made.\n- **Every `src/` file is one IIFE**, `(() => { const E = My3D2dge, SN = globalThis.SN; … })();`. It registers what\n  it exports on `globalThis.SN`. Top-level `const`/`let` would collide once the files are concatenated into one\n  script, and in the vm context.\n- **No file may touch `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` or `E.World3D` at top level.** Only call them inside functions that run after\n  the boot step. This keeps every file loadable in Node for the tests.\n- The dev page (`index.html`) loads the same files separately. Serve it with\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the dev folder (note the PID, stop it yourself). Day-to-day checks\n  use the bundle, which works from `file://`. The CDN imports work cross-origin: jsDelivr sends\n  `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, verified. Chromium treats file:// as a secure context. Wherever WebGPU is missing,\n  three.js falls back to WebGL 2 by itself.\n- `tools/bundle.mjs` also checks that the inlined hub client equals `vendor/aihub-session.js` byte for byte, that no\n  source contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX` or `placeholder`, and that no `<script src=` remains. It prints the file size.\n\n### 2.4 Units, axes, conventions (bugs hide here: unit-test them first)\n\n- **Engine units everywhere in the sim:** 16 u = 1 m (World3D's `U`). Speeds are u/s, accelerations u/s². Track data\n  is written in **metres** and converted once, at load (`×16`). Displayed speed: `km/h = v[u/s] × 0.225`\n  (= 3.6 / 16).\n- **Axes (the engine's):** x east, y south, z up. This frame is *left-handed*. World3D converts to three.js with\n  `toThree(x, y, z) = (x/16, z/16, y/16)`.\n- **Angles:** radians from +x toward +y (0 = east, π/2 = south). World3D's camera yaw uses the same.\n- **Frames:** for a forward vector `F` and an up vector `U`, **right `R = cross(U, F)`** with the standard cross-product\n  formula. Check: F = (1,0,0) east, U = (0,0,1) gives R = (0,1,0) south, which is the right-hand side when heading\n  east. Then `U = cross(F, R)`. Write `SN.frame(F, Uhint)` once and unit-test it on 8 headings.\n- **Bank:** a positive bank rolls the right edge down. A right-hand turn (heading angle increasing) gets a positive\n  bank. Banked `R' = R·cos b − U·sin b` and `U' = U·cos b + R·sin b`. Unit test: in a right turn the right edge is lower\n  (`z` of `C + R'·w` < `z` of `C − R'·w`).\n- **Time:** the engine's fixed step, 1/120 s (`update(dt)`). The sim never reads wall-clock time. `game.alpha` is only\n  for drawing.\n- **Randomness:** the sim uses `SN.rng(stream)` (wrapping `E.rng(seed)`), one stream per purpose:\n  `race.rng('pickup')` and `race.rng('ai:' + i)`. **Never `Math.random` in the sim.** Cosmetic code (particles,\n  confetti) may use it.\n- **Track coordinates:** `s` is arc length along the centre line in u, 0 at the start line, wrapping at `L`. `u` is\n  lateral, positive to the right (along `R'`). `h` is height above the surface, along `U'`. A ship's race progress is\n  `p = lap·L + s`.\n\n### 2.5 Modules and their public surfaces\n\nThe sim layer (Node-safe, deterministic): `00-core, 01-input (mapping only), 05–11 data, 20-track, 25-ship,\n27-collide, 30-weapons, 35-ai, 40-race, 45-replay`. The presentation layer (browser only, reads the sim, never writes\nto it): `50-audio, 55-glyphs, 60–68 render/fx, 70-hud, 75/78-ui, 80-dev (UI side), 85-attract (director side),\n90-hub (network side)`.\n\nThe sim talks to presentation through **`race.events`**: an array the race clears at the start of each step and fills\nduring it. Examples: `{ t: 'pickup', ship, weapon }`, `{ t: 'fire', ship, weapon }`, `{ t: 'hit', ship, by, weapon,\ndmg }`, `{ t: 'wall', ship, impact }`, `{ t: 'scrape', ship, k }`, `{ t: 'pad', ship, kind }`, `{ t: 'land', ship,\nangle }`, `{ t: 'roll', ship, ok }`, `{ t: 'lap', ship, lap, time }`, `{ t: 'finish', ship, place, time }`,\n`{ t: 'elim', ship, by }`, `{ t: 'respawn', ship }`, `{ t: 'rescue', ship }`, `{ t: 'wrongway', ship }`,\n`{ t: 'lock', ship, target }`, `{ t: 'zone', n }`, `{ t: 'drill', id, ok }`. Presentation reads the events every\nstep for sounds, particles, HUD callouts and rumble. Tests assert on them.\n\nKey APIs, as a contract the builders keep:\n\n```js\nconst T = SN.Track.build(SN.TRACKS.solenne)     // throws with a clear message if the data is invalid\nT.L; T.slices                                    // slice i at s = i*T.ds (ds ≈ 32 u)\nT.frameAt(s, out)                                // { C, F, R, U, w (half width), bank, flags } interpolated\nT.surface(s, u, out)                             // { P (surface point), N (normal), wallL, wallR, kind: 'deck'|'gap'|'drop', ceil }\nT.locate(P, sHint)                               // local Newton search from sHint -> { s, u, h } (never global)\nT.query(x, y, zHint)                             // 2D bucket lookup -> nearest slice with surface below zHint (particles, terrain, cameras)\nT.groundBelow(x, y, z)                           // the track surface z under (x, y) below z (via query), else -Infinity: World3D's particleGround\nT.line(s) / T.vline(s)                           // AI racing line lateral offset (u) and target speed (u/s) per class\nT.features                                       // pads, pit, tunnels, jumps, openEdges, sectors, grid slots, start\nconst R = new SN.Race({ track: T, mode, cls, laps, seed, entrants, rules })   // entrants: [{ pilot, team, control: 'human'|'ai'|'ghost', input }]\nR.step(dt); R.ships; R.events; R.state; R.results(); R.hash()\nSN.Ship.step(ship, controls, dt, race)           // the physics step (§3.2)\nSN.AI.controls(ship, race) -> ShipControls       // also used by the player bot, autopilot and attract\nSN.Replay.record(race) / SN.Replay.play(data)    // deterministic re-simulation\nSN.Tune.T.<key>                                  // live gameplay variables (§3.15), SN.Tune.reset()\n```\n\n`ShipControls = { steer: -1..1, pitch: -1..1 (+ = nose down), thrust: 0|1, brakeL: 0..1, brakeR: 0..1, fire: bool\n(held), firePressed, fireReleased, absorb: bool (pressed), shiftL, shiftR: bool (pressed) }`.\n\n### 2.6 Scenes (engine scenes) and the flow\n\n```\nboot steps ──► title ──(10 s idle)──► attract ──(any input)──► title\n                 │ (touch-only: notice + SETTINGS button; attract still runs)\n                 ▼ START\n               menu ──► setup (class → track → team/pilot → laps) ──► loading ──► race ──► results ──► replay\n                 │                                                                  ▲        │\n                 ├──► season (tiers, events, briefings, GP standings, ending) ──────┘        └──► menu / retry / next\n                 ├──► academy (a race mode) · time trial · hot lap · snipeout · overclock · versus (join P2)\n                 └──► how to play · settings (engine) · about/credits\nAIHub.active ──► session (own card ─► race ─► round over)   (no title, menus or attract)\n```\n\n- `race` is the only `pausable: true` scene. It is not pausable in a hub session. Its pause menu is the engine's.\n  **`menu.items` may be a function**: the engine calls it each time the pause screen opens and drops falsy entries\n  (verified in the engine: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`), so the items can depend on the\n  mode. It has: Resume (engine), **Restart**, **Camera** (cycles), **How to play**, **Dev mode**, Settings,\n  Controls, Full screen (engine), and Quit (engine, `quit: 'menu'`; `false` in a session). In Overclock and Hot Lap it\n  also has **End run**, which counts as finishing.\n- Every scene's `draw(r)` calls `world.draw(r)` for the 3D picture first (the active world: the hangar for menus, the\n  track for races and the title), then its 2D UI in `r.overlay`.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(s, u), … }` (§7) exposes the state for tests and `tools/check.mjs`.\n\n### 2.7 Engine changes (make them in M0–M2; mark each `// SNIPEOUT EDIT:`)\n\n1. **Button symbols default to PlayStation, and the option lives in Settings.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs`\n   (search `glyphs: { group: 'Controller'`): `group: 'Display'`, `value: 'playstation'`,\n   `options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox', 'Xbox A B X Y']]`.\n2. **Font glyphs ✕ ○ □ △** in both pixel fonts (the `G57` 5×7 table and the `GLYPHS` 3×5 table), so `E.font.text`\n   can print `input.label()` results. The pretty colored prompt icons are our own (`55-glyphs.js`). These are the\n   fallback inside running text.\n3. **Analog buttons.** In `Input.tick`, store `this.padValues[i] = b.value` and add\n   `input.value(action) → 0..1`: the largest over its bound codes. Keys and mouse give 1 while held. Pad buttons give\n   their `value`. Axis codes give the stick magnitude. Tests can set `input._valueOverride[action]`. Airbrakes use it\n   (L2/R2 analog).\n4. **Synth extensions in `Audio._voice` / `_note`** (search `_voice(p, bus, t0, mul = 1)`). Oscillator voices accept:\n   - `lp: { f, q, env, dec }`: a lowpass BiquadFilter at `f` Hz with resonance `q`, opened by `env` Hz that decays over\n     `dec` s (acid bass, plucks);\n   - `uni: n, det: cents`: n detuned copies (supersaws);\n   - `pan: −1..1`.\n\n   Music tracks pass them through: `{ wave: 'saw', lp: {…}, uni: 3, det: 12, pan }`. A note token ending in `!`\n   (`'A2!'`) is an accent: louder, and the filter env doubled. Add drum tokens `p` (clap: three 10 ms noise bursts and\n   a tail) and `b` (sub boom: sine 55→35 Hz, 0.4 s). Sidechain: `duck: .35` on a drums track dips the music bus by\n   that much on every `k` (a 120 ms release). The house and trance songs use it.\n5. **World3D particle ground.** In `World3D.draw` (search `g.particles.ground = (x, y, z) => this.level.floorAt`),\n   use `this.o.particleGround || ((x, y, z) => this.level.floorAt(x, y, z))`. We pass `(x, y, z) => track surface\n   below`, so sparks land on the track, not on the terrain 30 m below.\n6. **(If needed) logarithmic depth.** If distant coplanar geometry z-fights, pass `logarithmicDepthBuffer: true` to\n   `new THREE.WebGPURenderer` in `init`. Try polygon offset on the decal materials first (§3.7).\n7. **Hub rule: Create + Options.** In a session the race is not pausable at all (`pausable: false`), so Options never\n   opens our pause. No engine change is needed. Note it in the About screen.\n\nRecord every change and its reason in `SN.ABOUT.engineChanges` (shown in About and the exit interview).\n\n### 2.8 Determinism rules\n\n- The sim depends only on the seed, the track and class, the Tune values (snapshotted into each replay), and per-step\n  controls. Ships update in grid order and pairs collide in index order.\n- `R.hash()` is FNV-1a over the Float32 bits of every ship's `(p, u, h, |v|, energy, lap)` and every projectile's\n  `(s, u)`, the same algorithm as World3D's `hashNumbers`.\n- Each race records `{ seed, track, cls, mode, laps, entrants, tune, frames[] }`, with `frames` holding the human\n  controls per step. Pack them as Int8 steer/pitch, Uint8 brakes, and a bit field. Run-length encode repeated frames.\n  Replaying must reproduce `R.hash()` at the end (a test).\n- AI uses its own RNG stream. Rigs and particles may use `Math.random` because they never feed back into the sim.\n\n---\n## 3. Systems\n","numLines":380,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2083}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P8uV41NqkjQKujGG5VuufX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
