{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BaU6jEgpjotYsRShRaDNvS","type":"tool_result","content":"2500:  menu(name) {\n9105: *    page is recorded, Quit to title with a confirmation), the settings screen (Display, Sound, Comfort and the game's own settings, from game.settings) and the\n9163:class Shell {\n9172:   * - standard: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the\n9179:    this._sys = !!o.system; this._std = o.standard !== false && game.o.menu !== false;\n9210:  /** does this shell give the standard menu (the corner's menu button and game.menu() open it)? */\n8: * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\n9: * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\n11: * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\n14: * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\n18: * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\n23: * 5. FRAME ORDER (handled by the Renderer):\n28: * 6. TIME. game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or game.start({ scenes, scene }).\n33: * 7. LEVELS. Top-down / isometric / brawler floors: E.TileMap (walls on the ground\n38: * 8. SOUND, TEXT, MENUS. game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song);\n43: * 8a. STANDARD FEATURES come with every game (10b): a pause menu with settings and controls the player rebinds, saved\n49: * 8b. LOOK. Aim for PS1 / N64 era 2D, not NES: HD rigs (size, outfit, hair, armor),\n53: * 9. GPU LIGHTING is optional: game.enableGPU({ map }). Mark glowing things with\n56: * 10. SESSIONS. E.session.record(true) records every later page load (or ?record=1 for one): the frame times, the\n59: * 11. TESTS. new E.Game({ headless: true }) (the default in Node) runs with no screen: game.advance(1) runs 120 fixed\n92: * 1. MATH\n319: * 1b. MORE EASINGS  the rest of Penner's set, beside the core's (E.ease): in a section of its own, so a kit whose game eases\n340: * 2. PIXEL PRIMITIVES  (all snap to whole pixels, never anti-aliased)\n488: * 2b. SPRITE FRAMES, TURNS AND SWAPS  E.anim (frames to play), E.rotSprite (r.sprite's and px.sprite's rot: turned in\n541: * 3. VIEWS (projections)\n627: * 4. SCREEN  low-res buffer, whole-number upscale, sub-pixel camera on present\n739: * 5. INPUT  keyboard, mouse, gamepad, touch stick, on-screen buttons\n1302: * 6. PIXEL FONT  3x5 glyphs for in-world numbers and labels\n1455: * 7. LIGHTING  per-pixel darkness with dithered bands, lights measured on the ground\n1564: * 8. PARTICLES  3D, projected. kinds: dust, spark, bit, ember, ring, text\n1677: * 9. RENDERER  per-frame drawing API handed to your draw(r) function\n2241: * 10. GAME  loop, clock, camera, hit-stop, shake, slow motion\n2959: * 10b. STANDARD FEATURES  what every game gets without writing it, so a game's code goes to the game:\n3146: * 10c. BOOT  the start of every game, like a console's BIOS: the system checked, the renderer started, the game's own\n3294: * 10d. TWEENS AND SCRIPTS  motion and timing on game time (what Phaser calls tweens and timelines, Unity coroutines):\n3397: * 10e. PATHS  a line through points, measured by distance along it: moving platforms, enemy dives and waves, a race's\n3450: * 10f. TRANSITIONS  the ways from one scene to the next besides a fade: game.go(name, data, { fade: 'wipe' | 'iris' |\n3489: * 10g. WEATHER  game.weather = { kind: 'rain' | 'snow' | 'ash' | 'leaves' | 'sand', amount: 0..1 (.5), wind: -1..1,\n3581: * 10h. INPUT SEQUENCES  moves made of several inputs in a row (a fighter's special moves, a cheat code, a rhythm\n3627: * 10i. CAMERA WINDOW AND FRAMING  game.cam.window (a dead zone), cam.snapY (height on landing), cam.frame(targets)\n3654: * 10j. PLAYERS AND SPLIT SCREEN  two to four on one machine. game.players: game.input is player 1 (a game of one is\n3794: * 10k. SAVE SLOTS  game.saves: three slots and an autosave, each holding the game's own data with its place, time\n3918: * 11. HUMANOID RIG\n4861: * 12. BLOB RIG  (slimes, jellies) squash-and-stretch sphere that looks at a target\n4960: * 13. TILE MAP  floor texture baked per view, walls as extruded boxes, collision\n5641: * 13b. MATERIALS  one open registry for every surface: wall faces, wall tops, floors, platform tiles and the faces of\n5907: * 14. FLOW FIELD  BFS distance map toward a target: cheap pathfinding for crowds\n6020: * 15. TEXTURE HELPERS for floorTex functions\n6081: * 16. PLATFORM MAP  side-scroller levels (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania, Metroid).\n6503: * 16b. PLATFORMER  the side views' character body, as E.Mover is the ground views': run, jump (coyote time, a jump\n6618: * 16c. TRAVERSAL  the Platformer's further moves, each opt-in (today's defaults are unchanged), and the rig's poses for\n6847: * 17. BODY  ground-plane physics for top-down, isometric and brawler games:\n6897: * 17b. ACTIONS  timed actions of several beats that move a body along a curve and drive its rig: climb up (and down),\n7085: * 17c. MOVER  the ground views' character controller, what Platformer is to side views. Walking, running, sprinting and\n7299: * 17d. PERCEPTION  sight, hearing and suspicion, read through the height rules (E.HEIGHTS, map.ray).\n7363: * 18. BULLETS  a pool of projectiles for shooters, boss patterns and magic.\n7443: * 19. SPATIAL HASH  find nearby things fast (crowds, bullets vs many enemies)\n7471: * 20. AUDIO  a tiny chip synthesizer (no sound files): square, pulse, triangle,\n7826: * 21b. BACKDROPS  layered parallax scenery behind the level: sky gradient, sun or\n7984: * 21c. PROPS  procedural pixel-art set dressing, so rooms never look empty:\n8121: * 21e. SHAPES  still 3D objects written as data: named parts, each a primitive with a material, drawn from any view and\n8645: * first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: {\n8667: * 21g. SOLID SHAPES  a shape made solid in a tile map: map.solid(id, x, y, z, o), and the map's collide, groundAt and\n8716: * 21f. THE CORE LIBRARY  the materials and shapes every game has (docs/ASSETS.md lists them; the store, src/assets/, has\n8784: * 21. UI  window frames, bars, hearts, dialog boxes and menus (all pixel-drawn,\n9086: * 21d. SHELL  an HTML interface over the game, built from data: the screens (title, pause, settings, help, results, map)\n9588: * 22. GPU LIGHTING (WebGPU)  optional. game.enableGPU({ map }) turns it on.\n9928: * 23. SESSIONS  record a play session on any device, replay it exactly in a headless browser","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4110026b-2798-4dbf-ae8a-e81d8e816e30","uuid":"cf57d986-73d2-4425-a4e9-8490b268cbd9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:50:19.450Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"2500:  menu(name) {\n9105: *    page is recorded, Quit to title with a confirmation), the settings screen (Display, Sound, Comfort and the game's own settings, from game.settings) and the\n9163:class Shell {\n9172:   * - standard: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the\n9179:    this._sys = !!o.system; this._std = o.standard !== false && game.o.menu !== false;\n9210:  /** does this shell give the standard menu (the corner's menu button and game.menu() open it)? */\n8: * 0. EVERY GAME STARTS WITH  const E = My3D2dge;  and then uses E.Game, E.px, ...\n9: * 1. WORLD SPACE IS 3D. x = east, y = south, z = up. One floor tile = 16 units.\n11: * 2. A VIEW turns world points into screen pixels. Swap the view and the whole game\n14: * 3. CHARACTERS ARE RIGS, NOT SPRITES. E.Humanoid and E.Blob recompute their\n18: * 4. PIXEL RULES. Draw with E.px (rect, line, disc, ell, poly, sprite...). Every shape\n23: * 5. FRAME ORDER (handled by the Renderer):\n28: * 6. TIME. game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or game.start({ scenes, scene }).\n33: * 7. LEVELS. Top-do